{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_750e411d6b5a",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 88,
      "mechanism": "At the start of a new frame (cycle 5: tvalid=1, tready=1, tlast=0, frame_reg=0), the original code initializes frame_len_next to 0 then adds the byte count. However, because tkeep=1 is a non-contiguous mask (violating the artifact contract), the loop incorrectly computes bit_cnt=0, leaving frame_len_next=0. The patch adds a !tvalid guard so the 'new frame' branch is skipped when tvalid=0, but here tvalid=1 so the branch still executes. The observed failure (frame_len=3 vs expected 1) indicates the frame length is accumulating across cycles; this patch alone does not fix the overcount but is the only candidate that directly gates the frame-initialization logic with the tvalid signal present at the failure cycle."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_e04315a063a5",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 88,
      "mechanism": "Same line as patch_750e411d6b5a but adds !tvalid instead of tvalid. Since tvalid=1 at the failure cycle, this condition becomes false and would skip the new-frame initialization entirely, preventing the incorrect bit_cnt accumulation from a non-contiguous tkeep. However, skipping initialization also breaks legitimate frame starts, so this patch trades one error for another and is ranked lower."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_158c82e15fb9",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 88,
      "mechanism": "Adds !tlast guard to the new-frame branch. At the failure cycle tlast=0, so the condition passes and the branch executes, suffering the same bit_cnt=0 error. This patch only prevents re-initialization on a last beat, which does not occur here, so it cannot correct the observed overcount."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_f178291ef6b5",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 88,
      "mechanism": "Adds tlast guard (opposite polarity). Since tlast=0 at the failure cycle, this condition fails and would skip the new-frame branch, avoiding the bad bit_cnt addition. Like patch_e04315a063a5, it suppresses legitimate frame starts and is therefore a workaround rather than a fix."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_1d987f221cab",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 88,
      "mechanism": "Adds !tready guard. At the failure cycle tready=1, so the condition fails and the branch is skipped, again avoiding the bad addition but also breaking normal operation when tready=1."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_14a0bc3c436d",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 81,
      "mechanism": "Gates the entire transfer-handling block with tlast. Since tlast=0 at the failure cycle, the whole block is skipped, so no length is accumulated and frame_len stays at its previous value. This prevents the overcount but also drops all non-last beats, making it functionally incorrect for multi-beat frames."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_9ad7fbc1e607",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 98,
      "mechanism": "Gates the tkeep pattern match with rst. rst=0 at the failure cycle, so the pattern match is disabled and bit_cnt remains 0. The frame_len increment then falls back to the KEEP_ENABLE=false path (+1), still not producing the expected 1 because the new-frame initialization already added 0. This patch only affects the popcount logic, not the frame boundary logic."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_5e3af25f023e",
      "failure_port": "frame_len",
      "source_line": 81,
      "mechanism": "Inserts a reset of frame_len_next to 0 whenever frame_len_valid_reg is asserted. In the discovery trace frame_len_valid is never asserted, so this patch has no effect on the observed failure; it addresses a different symptom (stale length after a valid pulse) and is included only because it directly writes frame_len_next at the block entry."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The discovery trace shows a single-cycle mismatch at cycle 5 where frame_len=3 but expected=1. The inputs at that cycle are tkeep=1 (non-contiguous, outside contract), tvalid=1, tready=1, tlast=0, rst=0. The original code enters the 'new frame' branch (frame_reg=0), sets frame_len_next=0, then attempts to count enabled bytes via the tkeep pattern loop. Because tkeep=1 does not match any contiguous-low-order mask, the loop yields bit_cnt=0, so frame_len_next stays 0. However, the observed frame_len=3 indicates the register had accumulated 3 from prior cycles, meaning the frame_len_valid pulse never fired to clear it. None of the candidates correct the popcount for non-contiguous masks or assert frame_len_valid at the right time; they only gate various branches. The highest-ranked patches are those that gate the frame-initialization branch (line 88) with signals actually present at the failure cycle (tvalid, tlast, tready), because that branch is where the erroneous bit_cnt=0 is added. Patches gating the outer transfer block (line 81) or the popcount loop (line 98) are less targeted. All patches are workarounds; a real fix would require either validating tkeep contiguity or redesigning the byte-counting logic."
}
